course: french
lesson: 16
title: The body
level: primary-4
stars: 0
explain: The quickest way to learn the body in French is a game: in Jacques a dit, touchez la tête ! means "Simon says, touch your head!", and anyone who touches a knee is out.
explain: Every body part has its own gender that has nothing to do with you: a boy says ma bouche (my mouth) and a girl says mon nez (my nose).
explain: To say something hurts, use j'ai mal à: j'ai mal à la tête (my head hurts), j'ai mal au ventre (my tummy hurts), j'ai mal aux dents (my teeth hurt).
explain: Some plurals are odd: one eye is un œil but two eyes are deux yeux, and one knee is un genou but knees are des genoux.
explain: French sees hair as many hairs, so your hair is always plural: les cheveux.
fact: la tête ("TET") is the head; the ê sounds like "eh".
fact: un œil is one eye and les yeux ("lay-ZYUH") are the eyes; this is the oddest plural in French.
fact: j'ai mal à + body part means it hurts: à + le becomes au (j'ai mal au ventre), à + la stays à la (j'ai mal à la tête), à + les becomes aux (j'ai mal aux dents).
fact: les cheveux ("lay shuh-VUH") is the hair on your head, always plural; un cheveu is one single hair.
fact: le nez ("NAY") is the nose; the z is silent and the plural is also nez.
fact: une main ("MAN") is a hand, un pied ("PYAY") is a foot with a silent d, le bras ("BRA") is the arm and la jambe ("ZHAHNB") is the leg.
fact: The song Tête, épaules, genoux et pieds is the French "Head, shoulders, knees and toes"; un genou (knee) becomes des genoux in the plural.
fact: une dent ("DAHN") is a tooth; j'ai mal aux dents means my teeth hurt.
fact: avoir la tête dans les nuages means to daydream, like "head in the clouds"; avoir un chat dans la gorge, "a cat in the throat", means a frog in your throat.
fact: la bouche ("BOOSH") is the mouth and is feminine: ma bouche, even for a boy.
fact: J'ai les yeux bleus means "I have blue eyes"; the colour comes after yeux and takes an s because yeux is plural.
fact: Slang here: "ey up" (Yorkshire) means hey; "lush" (Wales) means lovely; "like" (Leeds) adds a shrug; "eh" (Canada) asks you to agree; "wee" (Scotland) means small; "heaps good" (Australia) means really good; "boss" (Liverpool) means great; "proper job" (West Country) means great; "y'all" (Atlanta) means you all.
exercise: In Jacques a dit, touchez la tête !, what must Archie touch? | his head | his knee | his tummy | his teeth
exercise: Lily says "les œils" for eyes. What is right? | les yeux | les œils | les œilles | les yeuxs
exercise: How do you say "one eye"? | un œil | un yeux | une œil | un œils
exercise: Josh's tummy hurts. What should he tell the nurse? | J'ai mal au ventre. | J'ai mal ventre. | Mon ventre est mal. | J'ai mal à le ventre.
exercise: How do you say "My head hurts"? | J'ai mal à la tête. | J'ai mal au tête. | J'ai mal aux tête. | Je suis mal la tête.
exercise: What are les cheveux? | the hair on your head | the horses | the eyebrows | the shoulders
exercise: How do you say "I have long hair"? | J'ai les cheveux longs. | J'ai le cheveu long. | J'ai les cheveux long. | Je suis cheveux longs.
exercise: How do you say le nez? | "luh NAY", the z is silent | "luh NEZ", with a buzzing z | "luh NOSE", like in English | "luh NAYZ"
exercise: What is une main? | a hand | a foot | a mane | the main road
exercise: How does pied (foot) sound? | "PYAY", the d is silent | "PIE-ed", in two parts | "PEED", with a clear d | "pee-AID"
exercise: What does genoux mean in the song Tête, épaules, genoux et pieds? | knees | toes | shoulders | elbows
exercise: Mia has toothache in more than one tooth. What should she say? | J'ai mal aux dents. | J'ai mal à les dents. | J'ai mal au dents. | J'ai dents mal.
exercise: What does J'ai la tête dans les nuages mean? | I'm daydreaming | My head hurts badly | I'm out in the rain | I'm very tall
exercise: What does avoir un chat dans la gorge mean? | to have a frog in your throat | to have a pet cat | to be very hungry | to be scared of cats
exercise: Harvey writes "mon bouche". What is right? | ma bouche | mon bouche | mes bouche | ma bouches
exercise: How does Chloe say she has blue eyes? | J'ai les yeux bleus. | J'ai les yeux bleu. | Je suis les yeux bleus. | J'ai le yeux bleus.
test: Archie's mate laughs, "Ey up, wrong bit," when Archie touches his knee. ("Ey up" means hey.) What would happen if the leader didn't say Jacques a dit? | You should not move at all | You touch your head anyway | You touch your knee instead | You must sing a song
test: Lily says les yeux is "lush but mad". ("Lush" means lovely.) What's the truth? | œil has a totally different plural: yeux | œil adds -s like every word | yeux means one eye | les yeux means the ears
test: The nurse asks Josh, "J'ai mal au ventre ?" Why au? | à + le becomes au | au means "very" | au is used for boys only | Josh should say à le
test: Jessica says, "It's just me hair, like, one hair-do." Why is les cheveux plural? | French talks about hair as many single hairs | Jessica has two hair-dos | It is a mistake in French grammar | Only long hair is plural
test: Riley says "le nose", then "Eh, so close." ("Eh" asks you to agree.) What should she say? | le nez, "NAY" | le nose, "NOZ" | le nas, "NA" | la nez, "NEZ"
test: Liam stomps his feet: "Easy-peasy, wee lemon squeezy." ("Wee" means small.) What is right for two feet? | deux pieds | deux pied | deux pies | deux piedes
test: Ava calls Tête, épaules, genoux et pieds "heaps good". ("Heaps good" means really good.) What is the plural of un genou? | des genoux | des genous | des genouxs | des genou
test: Mia tells the dentist, "Like, a lot, honestly." What does aux dents tell the dentist? | more than one tooth hurts | one tooth hurts | the teeth are clean | the teeth are new
test: Daniel says "Is his head in the clouds? That's boss." ("Boss" means great.) What does his French friend mean? | He is daydreaming | He is on a plane | He is very clever | He is feeling ill
test: Harvey asks, "It's my mouth, proper job, why's it a girl?" ("Proper job" means great.) What's the rule? | The gender belongs to the word, not to the person | Mouths are feminine only for girls | Harvey should use mon because he's a boy | Body parts are always masculine
test: A cartoon girl says "Mes yeux sont bleu." What's the mistake? | The colour needs an s: bleus | The colour should come first: bleus yeux | yeux should be œils | There is no mistake
test: ★★★ Your head, your teeth and your foot all hurt after a fall. Which sentence is right? | J'ai mal à la tête, aux dents et au pied. | J'ai mal au tête, aux dents et à la pied. | J'ai mal à la tête, au dents et au pied. | J'ai mal à la tête, aux dents et aux pied.
exam: Which of the following statements most accurately characterises the plural of the noun œil? | It is yeux, a form wholly unlike the singular | It is œils, formed by the regular addition of s | It is œilles, formed by the addition of -es | It possesses no plural form whatsoever
exam: Identify the correct rendering of "my stomach hurts". | J'ai mal au ventre | J'ai mal à le ventre | Mon ventre est mal | Je suis mal au ventre
exam: Select the response consistent with the established facts concerning the gender of body parts in French. | It is fixed by the noun and is independent of the speaker's sex | It is determined by whether the speaker is a boy or a girl | It is masculine for every part of the body | It is feminine for every part above the neck
exam: It is given that a person has blue eyes. Determine the correct French statement. | J'ai les yeux bleus | J'ai les yeux bleu | Je suis les yeux bleus | J'ai les bleus yeux
